/** 

@page benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole Single black hole test


Benchmark which solves the single black hole described in a CCZ4 simulation. 
While the output is not particularly exciting (the simulation should yield a 
stationary solution after a while), it is numerically challenging as we have
sophisticated boundary condition effects, a singularity in the centre which 
can lead to oscillations, need a scheme with low numerical dissipation, and 
so forth.


We therefore study three different numerical schemes:

1. A sole FV scheme. This one is very dissipative.
2. A sole FD4 scheme. This might be/should be subject to long-term oscillations
   i.e. numerically unstable.
3. A coupled FD4-FV scheme where we use FV around the black hole and FD4 
   otherwise. The fundamental idea is that we use a dissipative scheme around
   the black hole which eliminates oscillations or avoids that they pop up in 
   the first place. Everywhere else, we use a higher order scheme which gives
   us higher accuracy.


## Build executable


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python
export PYTHONPATH=$PYTHONPATH:../../../../applications/exahype2/ccz4

python3 convergence-study.py -cs 2 -plot
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

As always, the parameter --help makes the Python script yield further info.


## Clean up old and temporary files

The following statements remove all the glue code and autogenerated C++ files.
There is no risk in deleting them - if you rerun the Python script, they will 
all be reinstantiated.


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     rm *.o *.cmake
     rm README-*.md
     rm Makefile CMakeLists.txt
     rm Abstract*
     rm -rf celldata facedata globaldata observers tasks vertexdata repositories
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To get rid of the output files, type in

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
     rm *patch-file *vtu *.pvd *.bak
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~



## Visualise outcome

If you have set up your experiment via the Python script with the -plot 
command, you can use the standard Peano scripts to convert 
@ref tarch_patch_files "Peano's patch files":


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python
/opt/paraview/bin/pvpython ~/git/Peano/python/peano4/visualisation/render.py --filter-fine-grid solution-CCZ4SBH_FV.peano-patch-file
/opt/paraview/bin/pvpython ~/git/Peano/python/peano4/visualisation/render.py --filter-fine-grid solution-CCZ4SBH_FD4.peano-patch-file
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The picture below is taken from @ref page_exahype_coupling "ExaHyPE's coupling discussion" and
illustrates that the higher order solver covers the whole domain, while the FV 
is only active in a tiny cross-shaped region within.

@image html grid-setup.png




## Code remarks (FD4-FV scheme)

### Time step size

As we combine solvers, we have to ensure that their time step sizes match. In 
the context of FD4, this means that the FD4 time step size has to be calibrated
with @f$ p^{-2}=1/16 @f$ relative to the Finite Volume scheme. That is, if they 
have the same mesh size, FD4 takes significantly smaller time step sizes.
However, we want to havee the same spatial and temporal accuacy, so we need a
smaller mesh size for the Finite Volume scheme. The file
benchmarks/exahype2/ccz4/single-black-hole/convergence-study.py and the solvers
within applications/exahype2/ccz/CCZ4Solver.py hold further details.


While we recalibrate the numerical constant C to take the different spatial
discretisation orders into account, a simple rescaling does not yet ensure
that both the Finite Differences and Finite Volume solver run in-sync. The 
Finite Volume scheme will be subject to a higher numerical dissipation, while 
the Finite Differences scheme might run into numerical oscillations/instabilities.
Our aim is to remove the latter, so we can assume that the Finite Differences
scheme with its high order spatial resolution always holds the correct time 
step size. 

We therefore write a new benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::CCZ4SBH_FV::getAdmissibleTimeStepSize()
which ignores the variant autogenerated into benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::AbstractCCZ4SBH_FV::getAdmissibleTimeStepSize().
It instead asks the Finite Differences schemes for its admissible time step size
and uses that one instead. 


### Solution coupling

Even simple initial conditions can make our higher-order Finite Differences solver
fail: The higher order solution representation is not capable to resolve
shocks properly (or steep gradients), and the solution therefore starts
to oscillate. At the same time, experiments with Finite Volumes yield
solutions which a subject to very strong numerical diffusion. The
solution quality is not good. The black hole disappears.


There are two fundamental approaches to tackle this dilemma: On the one
hand, we can introduce artificial damping terms, i.e. numerical
diffusion, in the FD4 scheme. In the context of Finite Differences, this 
is usually done via Kreiss-Oliger (KO) dissipation. ExaHyPE's FD4 scheme indeed
supports KO term. In the present case study, we however switch them off!
Instead, we accept that both
FD4 and FV have their pros and cons and try to use each solver where it
unfolds its full potential. 


@todo Remove the KO terms here

Our coupling strategy follows @ref page_exahype_coupling "the default coupling schema" of
ExaHyPE. Different to the other coupling schemes, we however let higher-order 
solver cover the whole domain. As FV only will be used in a tiny region, this 
overhead is acceptable. Furthermore, we do not use any fancy colouring to 
decide which solver is active wher. We hard-code this information, as we know
where our black hole is. Since we couple solely PDE terms, we get away with a
volumetric coupling without any additional material or auxiliary parameters.
 
Our FV solver is, through various inheritance levels, a subclass of 
peano.exahype2.solvers.fv.EnclaveTasking which in turn inherits from
peano.exahype2.solvers.fv.FV. It is the routine
peano.exahype2.solvers.fv.FV.create_data_structures() which associates
a peano4.datamodel.Patch called _patch with the solver. This is the core
data per cell which hosts both the unkonwns and the auxiliary variables per
cell. 

We follow @ref page_exahype_coupling "localisation recommendations for solver coupling" and
overwrite the routines _store_cell_data_default_guard()
and _load_cell_data_default_guard() such that only data within a certain 
radius around the black hole are actually held. 
An analogous scheme holds for the faces.
All of these routines rely on the class functions
benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::CCZ4SBH_FV::isCellOverlappingWithBHImpactArea()
and 
benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::CCZ4SBH_FV::areAdjacentCellsOverlappingWithBHImpactArea(), i.e. 
I implement the actual logic within my C++ class and try to keep the Python glue code 
to a minimum.


Overall, our domain decomposition is similar to the sketch in the 
@ref page_exahype_coupling "generic coupling description", but the red-orange-yellow
part covers the whole domain.

@image html coupling.png


The image below visualises how the Finite Volumes are embedded into the overall
computational domain. This is very low resolution simulation. Therefore, the
central FV region is cross-shaped, whereas it will become more spherical as the 
resolution increases. It is taken from the code and also visualises explicitly
that the higher-order scheme overlaps the whole domain, while the FV scheme steps
in only in a few places:

@image html grid-setup.png


To realise our scheem, we have to plug into a few routines, introduce coupling
terms, alter some operators, and be careful in general with the ordering of 
the solvers. 

1. The Finite Volume solver is only active in its small local area around the 
   black hole. Consequently, we do not have to load or store data far away.
   We also should not compute anything far away. All data there would be 
   temporary anyway (as we don't load or store) and hence hold garbage.

2. The primary solver takes its reconstructed solution, i.e. the Q values within
   the patch plus its halo (of size 3 for FD) and projects this information 
   down into the FV solver. This should happen 
   
   - before the FV solver kicks off
   - using consistent FD4 data within the cell and on the faces
   
   I initially thought that the projection could be triggered by the FD4 solver
   after each time step. However, this is not a good idea, as we do not have 
   consistent face data at this point. We however need the faces to have proper
   halo information to be able to interpolate correctly. So it has be done 
   before the solver kicks off.
   
   Associating it with the FD4 solver's preprocess_reconstructed_solution would
   be convenient, as these data hold the halo information already, i.e. they 
   provide access to the patch data plus its halo. However, plugging into this
   routine 
   makes assumptions on the evaluation order: We have to know that the FV code
   has not started any calculation yet.  
   Therefore, this coupling has to happen within either solver's preprocessing
   action set as a preamble of its actual time step. 
   
   The problem with the preprocessing and reconstruction is that outer faces 
   around the FV domain will hold garbage. 
   
   @image html wrong-storage-scheme-with-enclaves.png
   
   In the patch at the front of this cut through the domain, the halos above 
   the top row of visible Finite Volume volumes did hold garbage, i.e. they
   illustrate this fact. This garbage propagates one cell down into the domain
   if we are not careful, then pollutes the data on the faces and consequently 
   transitions into the central area where we need an accurate FV solution. 
   
   Therefore, we reconstruct the FD4 solver's halo (temporarily), and use these
   data to project the solution down into a temporary FV patch including one 
   halo layer. We take the data from within this patch and copy it over into 
   the FV data. Now the FV patch data holds the updated interpolated data from
   the current time step. Next, we take the halo of one from this temporarily
   reconstructed patch and write it onto the faces of the cell. This way, we 
   ensure that they don't hold garbage. 
   
3. After the FV solver has updated its data, we can restrict it. However, we
   have to be sure that the FD4 solver will not overwrite these data.
   


